Exactly. Respect for the rules of a game is not necessarily respect for the people playing it.

The outlaw might be the most disciplined, honorable poker player at the table. He might insist on fair dealing, condemn cheating, and pay every bet he loses.

Then rob the winner outside.

And there may be no contradiction within his own incentive structure. He recognizes the authority of the rules inside a particular boundary, not necessarily beyond it.

That reveals several interesting distinctions:

* Rule compliance isn’t moral alignment. Someone can follow rules because they’re useful, not because they believe in the principles behind them.
* Trust is domain-specific. A trustworthy poker player isn’t necessarily a trustworthy traveling companion.
* Fairness can be instrumental. Even predators benefit from stable, predictable systems in which others willingly participate.
* Power determines which rules can be enforced. The table’s rules may govern the cards, while the outlaw’s gun governs what happens afterward.

But here’s the more interesting inversion:

The outlaw doesn’t even have to break the rules to take your money. He can respect every rule of poker while exploiting your mistaken assumption that those rules govern the larger relationship.

And this generalizes to business, politics, institutions, friendships, competition, and even cooperation.

It suggests that when evaluating someone’s character, asking “Do they play by the rules?” is insufficient.

A better question is:

“Which rules do they believe still apply when following them no longer benefits them?”

Because that’s where you begin distinguishing principled restraint from strategic compliance.

And perhaps the deepest layer: Who gets to define where the game ends? ;)